## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design and analyze non-classical logics using Kleene's 3-valued semantics with a novel XOR operator within a constraint network. It requires demonstrating systematic backtracking with unit propagation while handling partial information, and verifying consistency with unknown values in logical implications.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics
- XOR operator extension in paraconsistent settings
- Backtracking with unit propagation techniques
- Consistency maintenance with unknown values

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene 3-valued logic implementation
class KleeneLogic {
  static VALUES = { T: true, F: false, U: 'unknown' };

  static NOT(x) {
    return x === 'U' ? 'U' : !x;
  }

  static AND(x, y) {
    if (x === 'F' || y === 'F') return 'F';
    if (x === 'U' || y === 'U') return 'U';
    return 'T';
  }

  static OR(x, y) {
    if (x === 'T' || y === 'T') return 'T';
    if (x === 'U' || y === 'U') return 'U';
    return 'F';
  }

  static XOR(x, y) {
    return this.OR(this.AND(x, this.NOT(y)), this.AND(this.NOT(x), y));
  }

  static IMPLIES(x, y) {
    if (x === 'F') return 'T';
    if (x === 'U' || y === 'U') return 'U';
    return y;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
To gauge practical reasoning about logic constraints, Horn-SAT familiarity, and ability to reason about linear-time algorithms and incremental updates in data pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Horn clauses
- Forward chaining
- Partial vs full assignments
- Complexity O(n+m)
- Cycle handling and inconsistency detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function solveHorn(rules, facts){
  const trueSet = new Set(facts);
  let queue = rules.filter(r => r.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)));
  while (queue.length){
    const r = queue.shift();
    if (!trueSet.has(r.consequent)){
      trueSet.add(r.consequent);
      queue = rules.filter(x => x.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)) && !trueSet.has(x.consequent));
    }
  }
  return trueSet;
}
```

